  "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all real values of $x$ in the interval. So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=0,8$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =2\\sqrt{0+1}=2 \\\\ f(8) & =2\\sqrt{8+1}=2\\sqrt{9}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(8)=6,$ so the global maximum is $6.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14221929/assets/def7a0e41c8534710eec8bfa3c0f6795b5fbb20ea09058403046be5bef4594fb.png)",
